WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:20.320
JOSKOW: Good evening.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.380
Welcome to today's Council on Foreign Relations
meeting: "Power, Partnership, and Presence:

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:26.940
U.S. Navy Leadership."

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:34.560
We're pleased to welcome Secretary of the
Navy Ray Mabus to the-to the Council.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.820
This evening's session will focus on the secretary's
programs to reduce the Navy's reliance on

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:48.350
fossil fuels, grow its utilization of alternative
energy, and to pursue energy security in the-in

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:51.240
the face of global climate change.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:58.259
Secretary Mabus will first make a few brief
remarks, then I'll ask him some questions,

00:05:58.259 --> 00:06:04.400
and then we'll give the members of the Council
a chance to ask questions as well.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:05.400
Mr. Secretary.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:06.400
MABUS: Great.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.160
Doctor, thank you so much, and thank you all
for being here.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:15.620
The two questions I get asked the most are,
why does the Navy need as big fleet as we

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:20.960
have, and why in the world is the Navy worried
about energy?

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:25.560
The answer to both those questions is the
thing that the Navy and Marine Corps uniquely

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.419
give to this country is presence.

00:06:29.419 --> 00:06:34.410
There is no next best thing to being there-being
in the right place not just at the right time,

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.110
but all the time.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:40.669
So we are growing the Navy fleet after years
of decline.

00:06:40.669 --> 00:06:45.040
In 2001, there were 316 ships in the U.S.
Navy.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:52.300
By 2008, after one of the great military buildups
in our history, we were down to 278 ships.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:57.380
In that period, those seven years, the Navy
put 41 ships under contract-not enough to

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:01.780
keep our fleet from declining and not enough
to keep our shipyards in business.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:06.630
As of last week, I've been in this job for
seven years, so it's an exact comparison.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:13.570
I've put 85 ships under contract in the same
period with a smaller top line.

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:15.070
And so we're going to grow the fleet.

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:25.930
We'll have 300 ships again by 2019, and 306-308,
which is our demonstrated need-it's our assessed

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:26.930
need-by 2021.

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:35.720
And the second question: Why is the secretary
of the Navy, and why is the U.S. Navy, worried

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.550
about energy, about power?

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.780
And one thing is historic.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:46.040
We've always been on the cutting edge of energy
change.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:50.449
We went from sail to coal in the middle of
the 19th century.

00:07:50.449 --> 00:07:53.830
We went from coal to oil in the early 20th
century.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:59.260
We pioneered the use of nuclear for propulsion
in the middle of the 20th century.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:02.510
And every single time we did that, there were
naysayers.

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:07.199
You're trading something that's free, the
wind, for something that costs money, coal.

00:08:07.199 --> 00:08:11.710
Or, you're abandoning all those coaling stations
around the world for oil.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:18.650
Or, there is no way that you can make nuclear
small enough and safe enough to put in a submarine.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:21.830
Every single time, the naysayers were wrong.

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:23.990
They're wrong this time.

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:26.080
We're doing it for several reasons.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:32.150
One is-and the reason we started down this
path-is to be better warfighters.

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:34.710
Energy can be used as a weapon, and it is
used as a weapon.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.980
Look at what Russia did to Crimea.

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:38.209
Look at what Russia did to Ukraine.

00:08:38.209 --> 00:08:43.860
Look at what Russia tried to do to Europe
before the price of oil bottomed out.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:47.370
I did not want that used as a weapon against
us.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:54.320
Secondly, the cost of both price shocks and
supply shocks in energy.

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:57.500
Because oil is a global commodity, we don't
control the price.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:58.550
It goes up and down.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:04.450
But in the first couple of years that I was
there I was presented with more than $2 billion

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:08.180
in unbudgeted price hikes because of energy.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:16.220
And even in the Pentagon, finding $2 billion
that's unplanned for is not an-not an easy

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:17.220
task.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:24.620
The other thing is, we were losing a Marine
killed or wounded for every 50 convoys of

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:30.290
fuel we brought into Afghanistan, and that's
too high a price to pay.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:40.471
So, in 2009, I came up with some energy goals
that by-the biggest of which, by 2020, at

00:09:40.471 --> 00:09:45.500
least half of all naval energy afloat and
ashore would come from non-fossil fuel sources.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:48.260
We're there on our bases today.

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.500
We got there by the end of 2015.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:56.551
Half of the power that is under contract for
our bases-so, more than a gigawatt we have

00:09:56.551 --> 00:09:59.260
under contract for our bases.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:02.839
So we're five years early doing that.

00:10:02.839 --> 00:10:06.790
In the fleet, 30 percent now is alternative,
and it's growing.

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:13.010
We've got the Great Green Fleet at sea right
now, which is-the carrier is nuclear, the

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:18.209
surface ships where they're sailing on a blend
of marine diesel and biofuels.

00:10:18.209 --> 00:10:26.700
The Marines are making energy where they are
with things like very small solar panels that

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:32.860
are rollable-put it in your pack, power your
GPS, power your radios with that.

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:37.680
It saves a company of Marines 700 pounds of
batteries that they don't have to carry.

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:45.640
They are also using things like portable solar
for their-for their tents, for where they

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:47.630
live, for all their power.

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:53.390
Once you turn the generators off, number one,
you can hear if the bad guys are sneaking

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:58.720
up on you; and, number two, you take a target
off yourself because everybody knows where

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:02.280
you are if you've got a generator going.

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:08.569
But the second reason is climate change.

00:11:08.569 --> 00:11:13.750
As we move forward, the Navy and the Marine
Corps are our first responders.

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:22.000
So, as the Arctic opens up and is ice-free
now, and you've got shipping lanes, the Navy

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:28.730
is going to be the one to maintain freedom
of navigation through that as you get contests

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:35.080
for minerals in the Arctic, in the South China
Sea, off the coast of Africa in the Gulf of

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:36.080
Gabon.

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:39.880
It's the U.S. Navy that is the first responder.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:47.700
As sea levels rise and chaos and instability
follows, it's the Navy and Marine Corps.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:53.290
And the estimates are that in the not-too-distant
future, 100 to 200 million people are going

00:11:53.290 --> 00:12:00.209
to be displaced by this because 70 percent
of the world's population lives within 60

00:12:00.209 --> 00:12:03.870
miles of the ocean.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:07.980
And then we have bases on the ocean.

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:12.589
It makes sense that we do.

00:12:12.589 --> 00:12:18.390
And they're going to be threatened with rising
sea levels.

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:23.420
And so it's for a couple of reasons.

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:24.510
One is warfighting.

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:32.170
Two is just the effects of climate change
and our need to do something about it, because

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:34.260
it has a direct impact on our responsibilities.

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:41.550
And the final thing I'll say is, once you
do the ships and power to drive those ships

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:46.279
and to get those ships where they need to
be, and those aircraft, we have to have the

00:12:46.279 --> 00:12:51.020
sailors and Marines to actually fly them and
sail them.

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:56.810
And so the other thing that I'm extremely
proud of-and we'll, I hope, get into this

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:02.540
more-are some of the personnel initiatives
that we've taken.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:05.740
We were talking about it backstage.

00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:12.670
We brought Navy ROTC back to Harvard, Yale,
Princeton, Columbia, after an absence of 40

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:13.670
years.

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:18.360
But we also brought it to Rutgers and Arizona
State, the two most diverse campuses in this

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:19.550
country.

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:27.309
I opened up submarines and riverines to women,
and just opened up all ground combat positions

00:13:27.309 --> 00:13:33.689
to women, because if you set standards, if
you make those job-related, and you don't

00:13:33.689 --> 00:13:40.980
relax them, then to tell somebody you cannot
do it because of the shape of your skin or

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:45.440
because of who you love or because of the
color of your skin is just wrong.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:50.170
And we are a stronger force because we're
a more diverse force, because people with

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:56.090
different backgrounds think differently, they
bring different problem-solving skillsets.

00:13:56.090 --> 00:14:03.730
I tripled paid maternity leave for sailors
and Marines from six weeks to 18 weeks.

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:07.680
We're opening childcare two hours earlier,
leaving them open two hours later.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.710
And again, it's to make us a better force.

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:16.810
And I think that we are much stronger today
than we were a few years ago, when we were

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:24.769
excluding people because of their gender,
because of their sexual orientation, and because

00:14:24.769 --> 00:14:28.070
of the color of their skin.

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:32.430
So, again, thank you all for being here, and
I look forward to it.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:33.430
JOSKOW: Thank you.

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:34.430
Thank you, Mr. Secretary.

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:40.430
Let's stay on the personnel side of the equation.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:49.730
Have the senior military staff embraced your
alternative fuels and energy efficiency programs,

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:52.290
or have you encountered resistance from them?

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:58.140
MABUS: Well, when we first started-when I
first started, in 2009, we were hard-pressed

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:03.250
to find two flag officers that you could get
to endorse this.

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:12.320
We just did a conference on alternative energy
at National Defense University, and my energy

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:19.360
office did a film of flag officers endorsing
this and talking about why it was important.

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:29.060
And we had two single-spaced, typed pages
of flag officers wanting to be on the record

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:34.990
for this because they understand-because they've
seen it in action-it makes us better.

00:15:34.990 --> 00:15:35.990
It makes us better warfighters.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:40.740
So you've got the chief of naval operations,
you've got the commandant of the Marine Corps

00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:42.389
pushing for this.

00:15:42.389 --> 00:15:47.040
And, as important as that is, you've also
got that third class petty officer and that

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:52.639
lance corporal, because that lance corporal
doesn't have to carry the batteries, suddenly,

00:15:52.639 --> 00:15:59.610
and that lance corporal doesn't have to guard
a fuel convoy coming into Afghanistan or Iraq;

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:05.029
and that third class petty officer is figuring
out how to save energy on the equipment that

00:16:05.029 --> 00:16:07.000
he runs on the ship.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:12.029
And so it's a culture change, and it's permeated
the fleet.

00:16:12.029 --> 00:16:16.390
And it's because we really are better at our
jobs because of this.

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:21.759
And they're the ones that have to do these
jobs, and they're the ones that are on the

00:16:21.759 --> 00:16:23.769
tip of the spear doing it.

00:16:23.769 --> 00:16:27.350
They're the ones that see the impact the quickest.

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:31.970
JOSKOW: You count nuclear power as an alternative
energy source-

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:32.970
MABUS: I do.

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:36.550
JOSKOW: -and that's not true in all of the
U.S. states that have programs in this regard.

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:41.290
How important do you see nuclear energy being
in the-in the future, as you make this transition?

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:46.790
MABUS: Well, we are 17 percent nuclear at
sea.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:50.810
All our submarines, all our carriers are nuclear.

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:56.860
We tried it on other surface ships in the
'80s-cruisers, in particular.

00:16:56.860 --> 00:17:00.490
It's probably not going back there because
it's just not economically feasible right

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:01.490
now.

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:07.209
Oil has to be over about $240 a barrel for
an extended period of time to make it economic.

00:17:07.209 --> 00:17:15.900
The reason it is feasible for submarines and
carriers is that it's more expensive, but

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.919
it gives us a combat edge.

00:17:18.919 --> 00:17:23.030
The carriers have to be refueled ever 25 years.

00:17:23.030 --> 00:17:27.980
The submarines that we're building today never
have to be refueled, so you never have to

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:32.040
take them off the line to do that.

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:36.090
Even though it's more expensive, it gives
us an edge.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:39.920
And I don't ever want to send sailors or Marines
into a fair fight.

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:43.290
I want them to have an edge.

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:46.909
And I think nuclear power, particularly at
sea, does that.

00:17:46.909 --> 00:17:49.610
And, you know, I'll go one step further.

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:55.659
We're not looking at it onshore, but I do
think we should, at particularly distributed

00:17:55.659 --> 00:18:05.210
smaller things that-with some of the new technology
that's coming along, it's much safer, it produces

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:10.909
far less residue and nuclear waste.

00:18:10.909 --> 00:18:16.560
And, you know, it is an option that I think
we should-we should explore.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:23.950
JOSKOW: And a second alternative source are
biofuel blends that you're-have there been

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:31.330
any challenges in using the biofuel blends
in ships and other applications?

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:33.050
MABUS: No challenge about the fuels.

00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:34.900
I mean, we've got three requirements for biofuels.

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:37.540
Number one, it's got to be a drop-in fuel.

00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:38.750
We're not changing our engines.

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:40.480
We're not changing how they run.

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:47.110
So, for ships and aircraft-and we've certified
every single ship and aircraft on biofuels.

00:18:47.110 --> 00:18:49.120
Number two, it can't take any land out of
food production.

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:52.140
We don't want to be competing for that.

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:55.210
And, number three, it's got to be cost competitive.

00:18:55.210 --> 00:18:58.540
So we didn't have any challenges putting it
in the fleet.

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:01.030
I mean, the engines didn't notice the difference.

00:19:01.030 --> 00:19:04.580
The only difference we can tell is they burn
a little cleaner.

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:09.480
Engines last a little bit longer with biofuels
than they do.

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:16.810
But the challenge that we had, the first batch
we bought, in 2012, was a test batch-you know,

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:19.610
it was very small.

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:25.240
We sent it out to proof of concept to make
sure we could refuel at sea, that we could

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:31.350
refuel air-to-air, to make sure that we could
put it in our misnamed "oilers" and take it

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:33.480
to sea.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:39.730
We paid $25 a gallon for that because it was
a small batch and there was just no scale

00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:40.860
to it.

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:47.899
And so we got hammered for doing that, even
though first of a class, early adapters.

00:19:47.899 --> 00:19:55.060
I mean, smartphones are way more expensive
than rotary dial.

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:57.030
Computers are more expensive than typewriters.

00:19:57.030 --> 00:20:00.810
So technology sometimes is more expensive.

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:10.070
But, now that we've gotten to scale, the fuel
that we bought, that's now being used at sea

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:16.970
in the Stennis Carrier Strike Group and also
the carrier strike group that's in the Mediterranean

00:20:16.970 --> 00:20:21.000
right now, we paid less than $2 a gallon for
that.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:24.360
So even-and it was just a normal solicitation.

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:30.290
It wasn't, "Biofuel companies, give us your
bid."

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:35.940
It was, "Here's the fuel we need," and biofuels
companies bid, a mixture.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.320
And they were successful in doing it.

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:44.540
So even with oil at these very low prices,
it's competitive now.

00:20:44.540 --> 00:20:47.360
And so it's become the new normal.

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:52.010
JOSKOW: Have our allies' navies learned lessons
from what you're doing?

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:53.590
MABUS: They have.

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:59.600
We've got agreements with Australia, with
Chile, with Japan.

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:06.500
We're going to refuel, and we have been refueling-I'm
going in two weeks to Italy to-we're doing

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:08.010
an exercise at sea with the Italians.

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:09.770
We're going to refuel them with biofuels.

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:12.570
And they've got a biofuel plant now in Venice.

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:22.510
And Rim of the Pacific, the biggest naval
exercise in the world, every two years, we're

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:27.240
refueling the Japanese, we're refueling the
Chileans, we're refueling the Singaporeans,

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:37.370
we're refueling the Koreans, we're refueling
the Australians with biofuels, or with a mixture

00:21:37.370 --> 00:21:38.950
of biofuels.

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:48.440
And the first time we did it, on the USS Nimitz
at RIMPAC in 2012, the vice chief of the Australian

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:53.540
Navy came over by helicopter and we did a
big press event, and somebody said-asked him,

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:55.530
said, how committed are you to biofuels?

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:58.810
And he said, well, they're refueling that
helicopter I just came over on, I'm about

00:21:58.810 --> 00:22:00.030
to get back on it.

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.460
So I'm pretty committed to this-(laughter,
laughs)-to this whole thing.

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:11.599
And the last example I'll give you is, in
Singapore, there are two refineries.

00:22:11.599 --> 00:22:15.540
One's an oil refinery, mostly owned by the
Chinese.

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:20.020
Right down the road is a biofuel refinery
owned by a Finnish company.

00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:25.300
I don't want to be completely dependent on
the Chinese to refuel our ships in that part

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:26.300
of the world.

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:27.300
I want choice.

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:32.580
I want to be able to go somewhere else to
do it.

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:35.330
JOSKOW: It makes a lot of sense.

00:22:35.330 --> 00:22:40.740
Have you found any partnerships with the private
sector to be useful, or not?

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:44.170
MABUS: Well, we've invested in three biofuel
plants.

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:48.780
They come from-they've got completely different
feedstocks.

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:55.470
And they're going to come online by 2017,
and they'll produce about 100 million gallons

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:59.470
a year, and they have to compete in terms
of price.

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:07.030
But airlines now are-most of them are flying
on at least some biofuels.

00:23:07.030 --> 00:23:16.849
I mean, United has an investment in a biofuel
refinery, FedEx, UPS, Virgin, Alaska, all

00:23:16.849 --> 00:23:21.070
are flying on at least a mixture of biofuels.

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:26.440
And part of that is just-I mean, they're all
in the business of making money.

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:32.190
Europe has got carbon restrictions now, so
if you're going to fly into Europe, you better

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:35.720
lower your carbon footprint or it's going
to cost you-cost you some money.

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:39.710
But they also sort of see the way the world
is going.

00:23:39.710 --> 00:23:48.830
And I mean, as I said, these fuels, same chemical
composition, and their engines don't notice

00:23:48.830 --> 00:23:49.830
a difference.

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:56.602
And if you can create a more stably priced,
homegrown source of energy, and it-you know,

00:23:56.602 --> 00:24:05.190
it also gives another source of income to
people like farmers, which also is important.

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:13.429
JOSKOW: I know your-the size of the fleet's
going to increase first to 300, and then to

00:24:13.429 --> 00:24:14.940
306 surface ships.

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:21.810
Is that creating challenges, or is that an
opportunity to design new ships that really

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:23.900
accommodate alternative fuels?

00:24:23.900 --> 00:24:30.620
MABUS: Well, as I said, we're not changing
our engines at all.

00:24:30.620 --> 00:24:34.879
Now, what we are doing is becoming way more
fuel efficient.

00:24:34.879 --> 00:24:36.800
We're using a lot less oil.

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:41.180
We're using-the Navy's using 16 percent less
than we were seven years ago.

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:46.530
The Marine Corp's using 60 percent less than
it was seven years ago.

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:51.970
And so the-we've got two big-deck amphibious
ships, the USS Makin Island and the USS America,

00:24:51.970 --> 00:24:58.260
that are hybrids, the Prius of the seas.

00:24:58.260 --> 00:24:59.270
(Laughter.)

00:24:59.270 --> 00:25:08.250
They have an electric drive for speeds under
12 knots and regular gas turbines for speeds

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:09.970
over 12 knots.

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:16.571
The first time the Makin Island went out on
patrol, they brought back 40 percent of their

00:25:16.571 --> 00:25:20.880
fuel budget, and they stayed out 44 days longer.

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:25.510
But we're doing other stuff that's not nearly
as dramatic, but has a big impact.

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:35.020
If you change the lightbulbs on a destroyer
to LEDs, you save 20,000 gallons of fuel per

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:40.889
year on that destroyer, and you don't have
to change the lightbulbs but every seven years

00:25:40.889 --> 00:25:44.530
instead of every six months, and it's better
light.

00:25:44.530 --> 00:25:47.230
You can-you can actually see better.

00:25:47.230 --> 00:25:49.730
We're doing stern flaps and hull coatings.

00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:54.169
We're doing voyage planning like you did with
sailing ships, go with the-with the currents

00:25:54.169 --> 00:25:56.650
instead of against them.

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:58.680
And so we're cutting way back.

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:02.270
We're doing the same thing, but just using
much less fuel.

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:08.570
And our ships, while they're becoming more
energy dependent-I mean, they're energy hogs

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:09.570
now.

00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:15.889
The Zumwalt, the new destroyer that just launched,
is an all-electric ship.

00:26:15.889 --> 00:26:24.560
Our new weapons systems-the railgun, the laser
weapon-are very energy-intensive.

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:30.330
But we're being able to have more energy density
on these ships using less-using less fuel

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:31.330
to do it.

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:39.930
JOSKOW: And if we turn to your fixed installations
in the U.S. and abroad, is it the same strategy-renewable

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:42.690
energy, alternative energy, energy efficiency?

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:45.330
MABUS: We're doing all of those things.

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:48.440
And, as I said, we are over a gigawatt.

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:50.050
We're 1.1 gigawatts now.

00:26:50.050 --> 00:26:55.140
We use about 2 gigawatts a year on our shore
facilities.

00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:58.159
We've got two seagoing services, the Navy
and the Marines.

00:26:58.159 --> 00:27:04.740
But we also own 3 1/2 million acres of land
and 117,000 buildings.

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:08.670
So we're pretty big onshore too.

00:27:08.670 --> 00:27:11.419
We're doing it all through public-private
partnerships.

00:27:11.419 --> 00:27:20.600
We're doing it all so that private energy
companies can finance this over-that it's

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:23.740
financeable, that we do offtake contracts.

00:27:23.740 --> 00:27:26.970
Sometimes it's on our land, sometimes it's
not.

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:31.619
But we're doing it on virtually every base.

00:27:31.619 --> 00:27:37.040
And it's not just in California, it's also
in my home state of Mississippi, it's in Florida,

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:41.760
it's in Georgia, it's in the Northeast.

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:43.080
It's all over this country.

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:49.359
And public utilities have been some of our
best partners in moving toward alternative

00:27:49.359 --> 00:27:50.760
energy.

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:56.280
And what we're doing now is we're moving to
microgrids, so that if something happens to

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:59.740
the grid we can keep doing our military business.

00:27:59.740 --> 00:28:07.090
We can pull ourselves off the grid and continue
to do the defend and protect mission that

00:28:07.090 --> 00:28:08.090
we're supposed to do.

00:28:08.090 --> 00:28:09.370
JOSKOW: Well, it sounds like you're real leaders
in this area.

00:28:09.370 --> 00:28:11.879
I hope others can learn from it.

00:28:11.879 --> 00:28:16.619
Let me ask one final question before I open
it up to the members.

00:28:16.619 --> 00:28:22.330
You spoke earlier about rising seas and-as
a result of climate change, and you have bases,

00:28:22.330 --> 00:28:23.850
obviously, on the ocean.

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:31.170
Are you think about how you're going to accommodate
the changes in sea level in terms of where

00:28:31.170 --> 00:28:33.350
your bases are, how they're structured, and
so on?

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:34.639
MABUS: We're having to.

00:28:34.639 --> 00:28:42.720
Norfolk is probably the most at risk, and
it's not very far from having some real problems.

00:28:42.720 --> 00:28:48.110
We've already got the first climate refugees
in the United States now.

00:28:48.110 --> 00:28:55.960
An island in Louisiana disappeared, and it
was a story in The New York Times.

00:28:55.960 --> 00:29:00.840
They had to be resettled in this country.

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:03.429
And so it's not a theoretical thing.

00:29:03.429 --> 00:29:05.530
It's not something that may happen.

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:12.240
And you look in the Pacific, we're-you know,
the odds are we're going to have an existential

00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:14.820
event there that we're going to lose a nation.

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:18.590
It's going to go under and it's going to disappear.

00:29:18.590 --> 00:29:26.589
And a nation like Kiribati, which is about
a meter above sea level, you know, average,

00:29:26.589 --> 00:29:32.070
and one of the things that the president there
said is that one of his main jobs is to convince

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:36.780
people to leave, to move somewhere.

00:29:36.780 --> 00:29:38.139
This ought to concern us.

00:29:38.139 --> 00:29:41.101
This ought to-this ought to be a worrisome
thing.

00:29:41.101 --> 00:29:44.990
We ought to take a lot more action than what
we're doing.

00:29:44.990 --> 00:29:50.760
I mean, I think the president's done-has taken
some pretty bold steps here.

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:56.920
And one of the things the military brings
is we're big enough; we can bring a market.

00:29:56.920 --> 00:30:01.429
And we also have generally been leaders in
technology.

00:30:01.429 --> 00:30:09.850
I mean, the internet, GPS, flat-screen TVs,
most-a lot of our medical advances started

00:30:09.850 --> 00:30:15.510
in the military for military reasons, but
then migrated out to the civilian world.

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:17.520
And I think that that's happening here, too.

00:30:17.520 --> 00:30:21.850
JOSKOW: Let me follow up on that, and then
I'll open it up for questions.

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:24.139
Do you have a dissemination program?

00:30:24.139 --> 00:30:30.030
Are you trying to disseminate what you've
learned from this experience with the private

00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:32.669
sector-with utilities, with business, with
the residences?

00:30:32.669 --> 00:30:35.800
MABUS: Yeah, we're completely, completely
transparent.

00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:38.460
We'll share with anybody.

00:30:38.460 --> 00:30:44.470
And one of the things that happens is, if
you put it on base, it begins to migrate out.

00:30:44.470 --> 00:30:50.070
If you-if you show it works-like, we just-we
just had the largest solicitation for electric

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:53.589
cars in history.

00:30:53.589 --> 00:31:02.659
We've got over 50,000 non-combat vehicles,
and we drive them mostly on our bases, so

00:31:02.659 --> 00:31:06.570
electric cars are pretty perfect for that.

00:31:06.570 --> 00:31:11.889
But once you get charging stations on bases
and once you get charging stations near them,

00:31:11.889 --> 00:31:13.330
it begins to spread.

00:31:13.330 --> 00:31:19.600
And so that's-we're doing it overtly, but
we're also doing it just organically as well.

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:20.600
JOSKOW: That's great.

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:23.909
I'm sure you don't want a headline that says
"Navy Brass Buys $125,000 Teslas."

00:31:23.909 --> 00:31:24.909
(Laughter.)

00:31:24.909 --> 00:31:25.909
MABUS: (Laughs.)

00:31:25.909 --> 00:31:28.679
Yeah, you're not going to see that.

00:31:28.679 --> 00:31:29.679
(Laughs, laughter.)

00:31:29.679 --> 00:31:31.550
JOSKOW: OK, very good.

00:31:31.550 --> 00:31:38.850
Well, let me invite members to join the conversation
and to raise questions.

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:42.139
Let me remind everyone this is on the record.

00:31:42.139 --> 00:31:48.369
And we have a microphone that will come around,
so you raise your hand and I'll call on you.

00:31:48.369 --> 00:31:52.950
Wait for the microphone, tell us your name,
your affiliation.

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:56.399
And as I'm sure you all know, questions have
question marks at the end of them.

00:31:56.399 --> 00:31:57.399
MABUS: (Laughs.)

00:31:57.399 --> 00:32:00.540
JOSKOW: So questions and not speeches, thank
you.

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:04.640
Yes, in the-in the third row.

00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:05.910
Q: Thank you.

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:09.750
Very, very interesting remarks, Mr. Secretary.

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:13.150
You brushed upon it, but almost reluctantly.

00:32:13.150 --> 00:32:18.110
I mean, the Navy was the pioneer in nuclear
power.

00:32:18.110 --> 00:32:21.610
My God, it almost invented it.

00:32:21.610 --> 00:32:23.360
MABUS: Not reluctantly.

00:32:23.360 --> 00:32:25.820
If I-if I gave that indication, it was a mistake.

00:32:25.820 --> 00:32:31.859
Q: Well, I mean, it's the one source of non-fossil
fuel energy that can really move the needle.

00:32:31.859 --> 00:32:32.859
MS.

00:32:32.859 --> 00:32:33.859
: Can we have your name?

00:32:33.859 --> 00:32:34.859
JOSKOW: I think-yes.

00:32:34.859 --> 00:32:35.859
Q: I beg your pardon.

00:32:35.859 --> 00:32:37.790
Richard Huber, InVina Wine.

00:32:37.790 --> 00:32:41.260
We produce a nuclear fuel called wine in Chile.

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:43.010
MABUS: And we need a question.

00:32:43.010 --> 00:32:49.419
Q: I said: Would you-would you-why are we
unwilling to develop that source?

00:32:49.419 --> 00:32:50.740
You said 17 percent.

00:32:50.740 --> 00:32:58.760
It should be 50 percent of the-I mean, the
Navy could be the leader in developing this

00:32:58.760 --> 00:32:59.780
source of energy.

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:00.780
Why aren't we?

00:33:00.780 --> 00:33:09.609
MABUS: Well, I would argue that we are, in
terms of we-most of the-of the nuclear technology

00:33:09.609 --> 00:33:16.260
that comes out and is coming out now, most
of the research comes from ours-comes from

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:17.260
our research.

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:26.879
Now, there are differences because ours are
35-year reactors that aren't refueled.

00:33:26.879 --> 00:33:30.359
Civilian reactors are refueled, on average,
about every two years.

00:33:30.359 --> 00:33:32.300
And so you've got-you've got differences.

00:33:32.300 --> 00:33:37.250
Also, a submarine doesn't take as much power
as a-as a community.

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:43.750
But some of the technology is-a lot of the
technology is very transferrable.

00:33:43.750 --> 00:33:50.930
And as I said, one of the things that we are
looking at, but we haven't gotten there yet,

00:33:50.930 --> 00:33:55.460
is distributed nuclear on bases and things
like that.

00:33:55.460 --> 00:34:01.490
Not the huge plants, because in every source
of energy, regardless of what it is-whether

00:34:01.490 --> 00:34:08.889
it's solar or conventional energy-it's becoming
much more distributed, much more making energy

00:34:08.889 --> 00:34:13.370
exactly where you use it, and storing it where
you use it.

00:34:13.370 --> 00:34:21.310
And so, you know, we are-we're definitely
not avoiding it.

00:34:21.310 --> 00:34:27.609
Right now we can get, in terms of alternative
energy for our bases, you know, we are-as

00:34:27.609 --> 00:34:34.409
I said, we're past 50 percent and we're continuing
to go doing stuff like wind and solar and

00:34:34.409 --> 00:34:45.669
geothermal and hydrothermal, which economically
right now is cheaper.

00:34:45.669 --> 00:34:49.570
And it's-and it's, very frankly, easier, as
you know.

00:34:49.570 --> 00:34:53.290
JOSKOW: Yes, you were second.

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:54.899
Go ahead.

00:34:54.899 --> 00:35:00.760
Q: I'm Phil Huyck with Encite Corporation.

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:08.839
There is school that suggests that, long term-and
we know where we all are in the long term-(laughter)-Keynes

00:35:08.839 --> 00:35:09.839
put that very succinctly.

00:35:09.839 --> 00:35:10.839
JOSKOW: And he was right.

00:35:10.839 --> 00:35:14.470
Q: There is a school that suggests that fuel
cells, a hydrogen-based economy, is something

00:35:14.470 --> 00:35:18.400
that eventually we will come to in a time
for another range of fuel cells.

00:35:18.400 --> 00:35:22.820
As we know, solid oxide, proton-exchange membrane,
there's a range of technologies.

00:35:22.820 --> 00:35:29.260
What is your focus on, and what's the Navy's
perspective on, fuel cells as an energy source?

00:35:29.260 --> 00:35:33.619
MABUS: We've got several lines of effort on
fuel cells.

00:35:33.619 --> 00:35:44.480
One is for unmanned underwater vehicles, so
that there is a technology-it's still in the

00:35:44.480 --> 00:35:51.829
lab, but it's going to move out of the lab
I think pretty soon-that you mix organic matter

00:35:51.829 --> 00:35:55.420
with seawater and you make hydrogen.

00:35:55.420 --> 00:36:01.859
And so you can send down an unmanned underwater
vehicle; when it needs more power, it just

00:36:01.859 --> 00:36:06.960
sinks down to the bottom, scoops up organic
material and mixes it with seawater-of which

00:36:06.960 --> 00:36:13.390
there is a good bit in the ocean-(laughter)-and
it can stay out basically indefinitely.

00:36:13.390 --> 00:36:15.500
So we're looking at that.

00:36:15.500 --> 00:36:21.260
We have-we have been looking at fuel cells
also as power for our bases and our ships.

00:36:21.260 --> 00:36:33.200
A little farther away, frankly, than some
of the other technology, but certainly a promising

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:34.770
area in the future.

00:36:34.770 --> 00:36:42.750
And I have to say, I'm pretty neutral about
what sort of alternative energy you do-whether

00:36:42.750 --> 00:36:51.100
it's nuclear, whether it's fuel cell, whether-whatever
feedstock it is for biofuels, whatever-you

00:36:51.100 --> 00:37:00.339
know, if it comes from a landfill, if it comes
from camelina, if it comes from used cooking

00:37:00.339 --> 00:37:05.810
oil, all of which we've bought, I don't really
care.

00:37:05.810 --> 00:37:10.710
I do care about the carbon footprint, and
I care about the-about the fact that it doesn't

00:37:10.710 --> 00:37:21.490
take any land out of food production, and
I care about the fact that we can get it easily.

00:37:21.490 --> 00:37:25.970
I think fuel cells have a-have a very, very
bright future.

00:37:25.970 --> 00:37:32.070
I'm not sure we're quite there in terms of
the technology that we need for-particularly

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:35.490
to drive things like a naval ship, yet.

00:37:35.490 --> 00:37:38.990
JOSKOW: Let me try this side.

00:37:38.990 --> 00:37:39.990
Yes.

00:37:39.990 --> 00:37:41.350
Yep, second row.

00:37:41.350 --> 00:37:43.700
Q: Hello, Mr. Secretary.

00:37:43.700 --> 00:37:46.830
I'm George Stamas, old friend.

00:37:46.830 --> 00:37:52.630
We've spent 40 minutes talking about innovation,
extraordinary innovation by the Navy.

00:37:52.630 --> 00:37:58.550
I might add this new weapon that you've created
is also a source of great innovation, that

00:37:58.550 --> 00:38:00.900
we've all been reading about this weekend.

00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:06.460
Can you speak to the relationship with the
private sector, this spirit of innovation?

00:38:06.460 --> 00:38:09.240
And I know you spend a lot of time in Silicon
Valley.

00:38:09.240 --> 00:38:12.849
How do you keep up that private-that relationship
with the private sector?

00:38:12.849 --> 00:38:18.000
And what role do they play in all these amazing
innovations that we're hearing about?

00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:27.859
MABUS: Well, they play a critical and huge
role in a lot of this.

00:38:27.859 --> 00:38:32.570
The way we try to keep up is by establishing
the connections.

00:38:32.570 --> 00:38:42.530
It's also by making governmental acquisition
a little bit easier, a little bit less opaque,

00:38:42.530 --> 00:38:50.210
and trying to take the fear factor out of-out
of people selling to the military or to the-to

00:38:50.210 --> 00:38:51.800
the federal government.

00:38:51.800 --> 00:38:53.210
That's not easy.

00:38:53.210 --> 00:38:59.410
But we're doing a lot more pilot programs,
we're doing a lot more proof of concept, so

00:38:59.410 --> 00:39:03.970
that you don't have to jump through all the
hoops to do a program of record until you

00:39:03.970 --> 00:39:10.930
see-so you succeed fast or you fail fast,
without dragging on for years and year and

00:39:10.930 --> 00:39:11.930
years.

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:13.680
And so we do that.

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:23.710
We also have great partnerships with universities,
with Applied Physics Lab at Johns Hopkins

00:39:23.710 --> 00:39:30.869
or Penn State or Purdue or Arizona State or
MIT or-I can, you know, just keep listing

00:39:30.869 --> 00:39:34.869
them on and on and on-to use some of their
cutting-edge research.

00:39:34.869 --> 00:39:42.880
And a lot of it's funded from Naval Research
Labs, from other parts of the Navy or Marine

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:43.880
Corps.

00:39:43.880 --> 00:39:54.660
And finally, the Marine Corps twice a year
has just an open house-one time, once every

00:39:54.660 --> 00:40:01.770
six months at Quantico, and then the next
time in Twentynine Palms in California.

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:04.640
And they say, bring us your alternatives.

00:40:04.640 --> 00:40:08.010
Bring us what you're making.

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:11.950
And that's where we got the little solar panels,
the rollable solar panels.

00:40:11.950 --> 00:40:18.940
That's where we got the portable solar generators.

00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:27.021
There's a technology that spray can-you spray
something on a rock, put a lead to it, and

00:40:27.021 --> 00:40:29.819
it becomes a solar panel.

00:40:29.819 --> 00:40:35.130
We didn't know about that, but somebody brought
it in.

00:40:35.130 --> 00:40:38.359
And what the Marines can do is they can buy
it.

00:40:38.359 --> 00:40:40.690
They can buy it on the spot.

00:40:40.690 --> 00:40:43.019
Their Expeditionary Energy Office can.

00:40:43.019 --> 00:40:47.680
And so that's one of the ways that we-you
know, just show us what you got and maybe

00:40:47.680 --> 00:40:53.700
we'll-maybe we'll buy it, and maybe, you know,
we'll turn your small company into a-into

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:59.670
a much bigger company if it-if it helps us,
if it-if we've got a need for it.

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:05.369
And, you know, George, I don't think anybody
in here, when they think of Marines-and we

00:41:05.369 --> 00:41:10.599
got two of them on the front row here-they
don't think about ardent environmentalists.

00:41:10.599 --> 00:41:16.880
First thing-first thing-actually, we got three
of them on the front row here.

00:41:16.880 --> 00:41:23.700
But they are leading the pack in terms of
that partnership with the private sector in

00:41:23.700 --> 00:41:29.130
terms of finding what that new technology
is out there.

00:41:29.130 --> 00:41:38.430
And if that technology is something that we
can use, we're figuring out ways to get it

00:41:38.430 --> 00:41:48.069
quick and to keep companies from going under
just trying to get into the federal system,

00:41:48.069 --> 00:41:53.079
because Lord knows it's way too complicated.

00:41:53.079 --> 00:42:00.619
The last thing I'll say, at hearings the last
couple of years I've taken a chart, and it's

00:42:00.619 --> 00:42:03.089
about this big, and it's our acquisition process.

00:42:03.089 --> 00:42:04.470
It's the things you got to go through.

00:42:04.470 --> 00:42:07.579
It looks like a plate of spaghetti.

00:42:07.579 --> 00:42:08.579
And it's simplified.

00:42:08.579 --> 00:42:11.420
I mean, I didn't-I didn't put everything on
there.

00:42:11.420 --> 00:42:17.400
I mean, that is nuts that-the way we're doing
it today.

00:42:17.400 --> 00:42:20.140
We got to cut out a bunch of this.

00:42:20.140 --> 00:42:25.700
Congress has tried and, you know, we're trying.

00:42:25.700 --> 00:42:33.180
And as I said, we're basically trying to bypass
a lot of this and do pilot programs or proof

00:42:33.180 --> 00:42:34.180
of concept.

00:42:34.180 --> 00:42:39.450
I mean, the laser weapon, I talked to the
reporter who wrote the article about the railgun.

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:41.809
I talked to him this morning.

00:42:41.809 --> 00:42:45.670
He said that the railgun was the most-viewed
video The Wall Street Journal had done this

00:42:45.670 --> 00:42:47.390
year.

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:51.839
Last year, the laser that we've got out in
the Arabian Gulf was the most-viewed video.

00:42:51.839 --> 00:42:56.160
And the year before that, it was the X-47B,
the unmanned aerial vehicle that we landed

00:42:56.160 --> 00:42:58.009
on a carrier and catapulted off.

00:42:58.009 --> 00:43:03.720
He said, just keep inventing these things;
it's really helping us a publication.

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:04.880
(Laughter.)

00:43:04.880 --> 00:43:14.680
You know, that laser weapon, we sent it out
in 2013 as a demonstration.

00:43:14.680 --> 00:43:16.670
It's a 30-kilowatt laser.

00:43:16.670 --> 00:43:20.320
It was supposed to be for six months.

00:43:20.320 --> 00:43:22.590
They just loved the weapon.

00:43:22.590 --> 00:43:24.450
It's really valuable.

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:26.700
So we're learning lessons from that.

00:43:26.700 --> 00:43:33.180
It's still a pilot, but now we're building
a much larger weapon-the private sector is,

00:43:33.180 --> 00:43:37.819
having learned the lessons of what does it
do in a maritime setting, what does it do

00:43:37.819 --> 00:43:45.850
in, you know, 120-degree weather, how do you-how
do you keep it when it's not absolutely level,

00:43:45.850 --> 00:43:48.020
as ships tend not to be.

00:43:48.020 --> 00:43:55.730
And so learning those lessons has allowed
us to succeed way faster, and so when we go

00:43:55.730 --> 00:44:02.869
to a program of record we'll have a lot of
those questions that that spaghetti chart

00:44:02.869 --> 00:44:09.510
causes you to answer-we can skip big chunks
of that to get-to get more weapons like that.

00:44:09.510 --> 00:44:11.750
Same thing with the railgun.

00:44:11.750 --> 00:44:18.549
We're putting it as a test this year out to
see it if works, see how it works, see how

00:44:18.549 --> 00:44:19.700
it works in different environments.

00:44:19.700 --> 00:44:23.759
JOSKOW: I'll take another question from this
side, and then I'll move back to that side.

00:44:23.759 --> 00:44:24.759
Yes?

00:44:24.759 --> 00:44:27.099
Q: Secretary, thank you.

00:44:27.099 --> 00:44:28.619
Masazumi Nakayama, Citigroup.

00:44:28.619 --> 00:44:36.160
To keep Navy strong and keep seamen highly
motivated, what should U.S. taxpayer should

00:44:36.160 --> 00:44:40.700
do in terms of investment of their taxpayers'
money?

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:50.660
And also, what kind of points that we-American
should be careful and sensitive to the U.S.

00:44:50.660 --> 00:44:52.170
Navy?

00:44:52.170 --> 00:44:59.599
MABUS: Well, part of my job, I think, is to
protect taxpayer money.

00:44:59.599 --> 00:45:03.640
And I'll give you some examples.

00:45:03.640 --> 00:45:08.349
On ships, Littoral Combat Ship was costing
about 800 million (dollars) when I came in.

00:45:08.349 --> 00:45:13.319
It's costing 350 million (dollars) a ship
today.

00:45:13.319 --> 00:45:17.500
We're saving $300 million per destroyer that
we're building.

00:45:17.500 --> 00:45:25.009
We bought 10 Virginia-class submarines over
a five-year period; we paid for nine.

00:45:25.009 --> 00:45:29.420
You know, it's like having a punch card: "buy
nine submarines, get your 10th one free."

00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:30.420
(Laughter.)

00:45:30.420 --> 00:45:36.900
And it's because we're doing stuff like firm
fixed-price contracts and multiyears and stable

00:45:36.900 --> 00:45:39.960
designs and mature technology.

00:45:39.960 --> 00:45:45.730
And so those shipyards that are building the
submarines knew how many people they were

00:45:45.730 --> 00:45:48.599
going to have to hire and have over the next
five years.

00:45:48.599 --> 00:45:50.369
We gave them some stability.

00:45:50.369 --> 00:45:54.599
They knew what materials they needed to buy
for 10 submarines, and it's cheaper to buy

00:45:54.599 --> 00:45:56.880
them for 10 than it is for one.

00:45:56.880 --> 00:45:59.819
So we've been driving down the cost.

00:45:59.819 --> 00:46:07.779
And I go back to-and we haven't done it at
the expense-we're building a lot more ships

00:46:07.779 --> 00:46:10.509
for less money.

00:46:10.509 --> 00:46:13.819
We haven't done it at the expense of aircraft.

00:46:13.819 --> 00:46:20.770
We're buying 35 percent more aircraft than
we were in the previous seven years.

00:46:20.770 --> 00:46:29.119
But I go back, and my father was maybe the
cheapest human being who's ever lived.

00:46:29.119 --> 00:46:30.119
(Laughter.)

00:46:30.119 --> 00:46:36.200
And I ran for-my first public office was state
auditor, and I was campaigning in Wiggins,

00:46:36.200 --> 00:46:37.200
Mississippi.

00:46:37.200 --> 00:46:41.059
And I ran into this guy, and he looked at
me and he said, are you Raymond Mabus's son?

00:46:41.059 --> 00:46:43.190
And I said, yes, sir, I am.

00:46:43.190 --> 00:46:45.619
And he said, well, I'm going to vote for you.

00:46:45.619 --> 00:46:49.569
If you're half as cheap with the state's money
as your father is with his-(laughter)-we'll

00:46:49.569 --> 00:46:51.329
be OK.

00:46:51.329 --> 00:46:53.660
And so I'm just cheap.

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:58.951
I mean, I want to-I want to squeeze every
nickel till we get it.

00:46:58.951 --> 00:47:08.400
But the reason we need a great Navy, 90 percent
of the world's trade travels by sea-90 percent.

00:47:08.400 --> 00:47:13.119
Ninety-five percent of the data-when you're
looking at your smartphone, when you're on

00:47:13.119 --> 00:47:17.109
the internet, 95 percent of the data goes
under the sea.

00:47:17.109 --> 00:47:23.859
The reason the world's economy is doing as
well as it is today is the United States Navy,

00:47:23.859 --> 00:47:29.369
because for the first time in history for
the last 70 years we have had a dominant Navy

00:47:29.369 --> 00:47:36.430
and we've kept the sea lanes open for everybody-not
just those flying our flag or the flags of

00:47:36.430 --> 00:47:40.089
our allies, but for everybody.

00:47:40.089 --> 00:47:48.220
That's the reason the economy of the world
is as-is as a strong as it is today.

00:47:48.220 --> 00:48:00.690
And so if you live in Iowa or Uganda-you know,
you're completely landlocked-you're dependent

00:48:00.690 --> 00:48:08.029
on the sea, and you're dependent today on
the United States Navy for freedom of navigation,

00:48:08.029 --> 00:48:10.160
for freedom of access.

00:48:10.160 --> 00:48:13.109
And we're the only people who can do it.

00:48:13.109 --> 00:48:18.859
And that's why you need a great Navy, is that
presence.

00:48:18.859 --> 00:48:19.859
JOSKOW: OK.

00:48:19.859 --> 00:48:23.499
Yes, young woman right-she's had her hand
up.

00:48:23.499 --> 00:48:25.359
Q: Hello, Mr. Secretary.

00:48:25.359 --> 00:48:28.549
My name is Kassia Yanosek from McKinsey and
Company.

00:48:28.549 --> 00:48:34.279
My question's actually about conventional
energy, you know, and particularly natural

00:48:34.279 --> 00:48:35.279
gas.

00:48:35.279 --> 00:48:38.730
We actually have seen this huge renaissance
of natural gas.

00:48:38.730 --> 00:48:44.880
We have decades of it at-you know, that can
be developed at quite cheap, you know, numbers.

00:48:44.880 --> 00:48:48.999
And further, we've seen a globalization of
gas, and we keep finding lots and lots of

00:48:48.999 --> 00:48:49.999
gas offshore.

00:48:49.999 --> 00:48:53.950
So I'm wondering where it fits into the Navy's
strategy, particularly as you think about,

00:48:53.950 --> 00:48:55.730
you know, electrifying your fleet.

00:48:55.730 --> 00:48:56.990
Thank you.

00:48:56.990 --> 00:49:03.120
MABUS: Well, first, we can't use natural gas
on our ships or our aircraft because we've

00:49:03.120 --> 00:49:04.440
got the engines.

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:11.191
We've got most of the fleet we're going to
have, and we're building to the-to the fleet

00:49:11.191 --> 00:49:14.490
size and our aircraft, none of it can use
natural gas.

00:49:14.490 --> 00:49:18.970
I mean, it would be prohibitively expensive
to go back and retrofit it.

00:49:18.970 --> 00:49:24.019
That's why we're looking for a drop-in fuel
instead of something like natural gas.

00:49:24.019 --> 00:49:30.410
On our bases, we have moved to natural gas,
but I don't count it as an alternative fuel.

00:49:30.410 --> 00:49:32.800
It's still got carbon.

00:49:32.800 --> 00:49:36.119
It's still a non-renewable resource.

00:49:36.119 --> 00:49:41.700
And we're finding more, and that's great.

00:49:41.700 --> 00:49:44.240
And we're-and it's cheap, and that's great.

00:49:44.240 --> 00:49:46.910
But it's also, as you pointed out, globalized.

00:49:46.910 --> 00:49:53.329
It's a much more global commodity-not as much
as oil, but certainly up there.

00:49:53.329 --> 00:50:00.119
We're not now, but could be susceptible to
price shocks there, and supply shocks, in

00:50:00.119 --> 00:50:03.009
the same way we are with oil.

00:50:03.009 --> 00:50:11.579
So I think that it is a valuable thing to
use, particularly in our shore establishments.

00:50:11.579 --> 00:50:18.710
One of the ways we're using it is we're taking
out some very old, very inefficient power

00:50:18.710 --> 00:50:24.999
plants on our bases and replacing them with
much smaller distributed power on those bases,

00:50:24.999 --> 00:50:29.619
and powering them with natural gas.

00:50:29.619 --> 00:50:37.759
But I think long term, if we are going to
be better warfighters-you got to-you got to

00:50:37.759 --> 00:50:40.089
ship in natural gas.

00:50:40.089 --> 00:50:42.830
You've got to move it from one place to the
other.

00:50:42.830 --> 00:50:48.250
And I think it's much more valuable to be
able to make energy where you are, and it

00:50:48.250 --> 00:50:53.220
makes-it's a military advantage to being able
to make energy where you are instead of having

00:50:53.220 --> 00:50:57.099
to move it any amount of distance.

00:50:57.099 --> 00:50:58.750
JOSKOW: Yes?

00:50:58.750 --> 00:51:00.410
You're next.

00:51:00.410 --> 00:51:03.710
Q: Thanks, Mr. Secretary.

00:51:03.710 --> 00:51:06.240
It's Aryeh Bourkoff from LionTree.

00:51:06.240 --> 00:51:09.740
Been a fascinating talk so far, so thank you.

00:51:09.740 --> 00:51:15.960
There's a lot of criticism around the dysfunctionality
of government from a management perspective.

00:51:15.960 --> 00:51:20.079
But given the fact that you've been at your
helm for seven years, talk a little bit about

00:51:20.079 --> 00:51:27.380
not just on the innovation side, but how you
manage the scale of the Navy-the people, the

00:51:27.380 --> 00:51:32.349
way you structure it, the way you get through
complex issues, problem solving, et cetera,

00:51:32.349 --> 00:51:36.710
and why it's different from a lot of other
people's perspectives of government.

00:51:36.710 --> 00:51:37.839
Thanks.

00:51:37.839 --> 00:51:48.950
MABUS: Well, the Navy has about $170 billion
budget, Department of the Navy; 900,000 people.

00:51:48.950 --> 00:51:55.749
If we were a private company, we'd be second-biggest
in terms of employees only after Walmart.

00:51:55.749 --> 00:52:00.029
We'd be third-biggest in terms of assets,
between ExxonMobil and Berkshire Hathaway.

00:52:00.029 --> 00:52:04.849
We'd be fifth-biggest in terms of budget authority.

00:52:04.849 --> 00:52:09.119
And so it's a huge organization.

00:52:09.119 --> 00:52:14.829
And one of the things I've learned in various
jobs-being governor, being an ambassador,

00:52:14.829 --> 00:52:24.539
being in the private sector, being a CEO-is
that if you try to just dictate, if you try

00:52:24.539 --> 00:52:30.680
to say we're doing it this way, nothing much
happens.

00:52:30.680 --> 00:52:34.230
And so you've got to-you've got to give people
a reason to do it.

00:52:34.230 --> 00:52:38.220
You've got to show them how it makes their
jobs better or their lives better.

00:52:38.220 --> 00:52:42.800
And you've got to get complete buy-in from
the folks that work with you.

00:52:42.800 --> 00:52:47.109
And it's particularly true in government because-you
know, I'm a political appointee.

00:52:47.109 --> 00:52:52.770
I'm going to be gone, in fact, at the end
of this administration.

00:52:52.770 --> 00:52:57.710
There are not many certainties in politics,
but that's one of the certainties, is-(laughter).

00:52:57.710 --> 00:53:00.249
This was the most-requested job in the Obama
administration.

00:53:00.249 --> 00:53:05.270
Whoever gets elected president next is going
to have a line out the door to take this job,

00:53:05.270 --> 00:53:07.000
and it's a great job.

00:53:07.000 --> 00:53:17.160
But the military in uniform, the civilian
workforce, they're not political, and they're

00:53:17.160 --> 00:53:19.640
going to be there long after you leave.

00:53:19.640 --> 00:53:26.080
So if you just try to say do this, do that,
nothing much happens.

00:53:26.080 --> 00:53:30.809
And so you've got to-I mean, you got to show
them why it makes a difference.

00:53:30.809 --> 00:53:35.309
In energy, it makes a difference because it
makes you better warfighters.

00:53:35.309 --> 00:53:38.920
It affects our responsibilities-climate change
affects our responsibilities.

00:53:38.920 --> 00:53:45.420
In terms of personnel management, you know,
we're an all-volunteer force.

00:53:45.420 --> 00:53:49.140
We've got to recruit and we got to keep great
people.

00:53:49.140 --> 00:53:55.990
We can't do it unless we-unless we treat them
right, unless we make their careers more flexible,

00:53:55.990 --> 00:54:02.839
unless we-unless we give them more say, unless
we promote more on merit and less on time,

00:54:02.839 --> 00:54:05.740
for example.

00:54:05.740 --> 00:54:20.819
On things like acquisitions, you have to set
up systems so that-we were spending $40 billion

00:54:20.819 --> 00:54:23.450
a year on contracts.

00:54:23.450 --> 00:54:28.859
And so we set up a system called contract
courts, that said every contracting officer

00:54:28.859 --> 00:54:32.829
every year has to bring in all their contracts
and defend them.

00:54:32.829 --> 00:54:35.180
Do you still need it?

00:54:35.180 --> 00:54:36.309
Have you bid it out lately?

00:54:36.309 --> 00:54:39.319
You know, what are we doing with it?

00:54:39.319 --> 00:54:45.210
I mean, these are everything from cutting
the grass to building carriers.

00:54:45.210 --> 00:54:53.489
And so the first couple of years, we saved
$4 billion-10 percent-on that just by asking

00:54:53.489 --> 00:54:55.380
people, why are you doing this?

00:54:55.380 --> 00:54:58.960
What does it-what does it bring you?

00:54:58.960 --> 00:55:06.970
And one of the results has been that people
are now bringing stuff up that they see.

00:55:06.970 --> 00:55:10.779
You've got to-you've got to try to change
the culture.

00:55:10.779 --> 00:55:14.500
You've got to try to change the outlook.

00:55:14.500 --> 00:55:24.789
And the only way you do that is by having
policies or programs that do make a difference.

00:55:24.789 --> 00:55:28.049
Not doing too many of them.

00:55:28.049 --> 00:55:33.450
I mean, the scope of the ability to change
is pretty limited.

00:55:33.450 --> 00:55:37.410
I mean, I was governor of the poorest state
in the nation.

00:55:37.410 --> 00:55:43.339
Every day I got up, there were about a thousand
things I could do, I think, to make life better.

00:55:43.339 --> 00:55:47.180
If I tried all thousand, I was going to fail
at each one.

00:55:47.180 --> 00:55:52.660
You got to-you got to pick three or four things
and just be relentless about that.

00:55:52.660 --> 00:55:58.319
And Tom Oppel is sitting here, my chief of
staff, who was my communications director

00:55:58.319 --> 00:55:59.519
when I was governor.

00:55:59.519 --> 00:56:06.309
And, you know, just repetition is the key
to clear communications.

00:56:06.309 --> 00:56:13.300
You just got to just do it over and over and
over again, and explain it over and over and

00:56:13.300 --> 00:56:19.430
over again, not assume that just because you're
inside it every day that everybody else is

00:56:19.430 --> 00:56:25.779
paying attention, or cares, and that you got
to do your job and explain.

00:56:25.779 --> 00:56:31.559
JOSKOW: Right here, and then I'll come back
to Padma (sp).

00:56:31.559 --> 00:56:38.859
Q: You really answered the-you really answered
the question about leadership, which I was

00:56:38.859 --> 00:56:41.140
going to ask.

00:56:41.140 --> 00:56:42.140
Thank you.

00:56:42.140 --> 00:56:43.140
MABUS: Thanks.

00:56:43.140 --> 00:56:44.140
JOSKOW: Thank you.

00:56:44.140 --> 00:56:45.140
Padma (sp)?

00:56:45.140 --> 00:56:46.140
Q: Thank you.

00:56:46.140 --> 00:56:53.549
How does our totally efficient naval fleet
allow us in dealing with antagonistic activities,

00:56:53.549 --> 00:57:03.590
such as the Chinese building islands in the
waters of South China Sea?

00:57:03.590 --> 00:57:09.519
Does it help us better to deal with these
activities, or does it involve a military

00:57:09.519 --> 00:57:10.519
conflict, or?

00:57:10.519 --> 00:57:16.759
MABUS: Well, the way it helps us deal with
any potential adversary or anything like that

00:57:16.759 --> 00:57:19.749
is it allows us to be there.

00:57:19.749 --> 00:57:25.109
And these freedom of navigation exercises
that we're doing, we're going to sail where

00:57:25.109 --> 00:57:28.599
we are allowed to under international law.

00:57:28.599 --> 00:57:36.289
We think that the status quo should not be
changed unilaterally or by force.

00:57:36.289 --> 00:57:43.809
And by being-number one, by having the ships,
just having the ships to be everywhere that

00:57:43.809 --> 00:57:51.369
we need to be to do these exercises and these
demonstrations everywhere, but then having

00:57:51.369 --> 00:57:59.440
the power, the energy to keep them at sea,
to be everywhere, to be there when we need

00:57:59.440 --> 00:58:05.559
them to be there-and we need them to be there
all the time-that makes us.

00:58:05.559 --> 00:58:06.930
It's the presence.

00:58:06.930 --> 00:58:08.180
It's the presence argument.

00:58:08.180 --> 00:58:13.829
That's why, you know, I started out by saying
that's what we uniquely give the country.

00:58:13.829 --> 00:58:20.999
So it doesn't matter what the threat is, or
it doesn't matter what the issue is.

00:58:20.999 --> 00:58:24.109
Being there-there's no second-best thing to
being there.

00:58:24.109 --> 00:58:28.490
Ships in port in the United States don't do
us much good.

00:58:28.490 --> 00:58:33.220
You have to have them, and it can't just be
in one sea or one ocean.

00:58:33.220 --> 00:58:36.019
We have to have them everywhere, all the time.

00:58:36.019 --> 00:58:42.200
We're the only global navy and we're the only
one who can do this, and we're the only one-we're

00:58:42.200 --> 00:58:44.279
the ones that people look to.

00:58:44.279 --> 00:58:49.800
And so that's why we need the number of ships,
and that's why they need to be at sea.

00:58:49.800 --> 00:58:51.510
JOSKOW: Yes?

00:58:51.510 --> 00:58:54.930
Q: Me or him?

00:58:54.930 --> 00:58:57.490
JOSKOW: Yes, him.

00:58:57.490 --> 00:58:58.490
(Laughter.)

00:58:58.490 --> 00:59:02.619
Q: Herbert Levin, Council member.

00:59:02.619 --> 00:59:10.599
From a standpoint of the national well-being
is it more important that the Navy have more

00:59:10.599 --> 00:59:17.690
potent guns or that high-school graduates
in Mississippi can go to MIT paid for?

00:59:17.690 --> 00:59:24.640
MABUS: Well, I don't think that's a choice
we ought to make.

00:59:24.640 --> 00:59:28.150
I think you ought to do both.

00:59:28.150 --> 00:59:29.650
Q: Raise taxes.

00:59:29.650 --> 00:59:32.910
MABUS: Well, let me-let me-let me tell you
something.

00:59:32.910 --> 00:59:35.440
Let me give you a statistic that's going to
frighten you.

00:59:35.440 --> 00:59:38.330
It does me.

00:59:38.330 --> 00:59:44.999
Three out of four Americans age 18 to 24 do
not qualify for the U.S. military-three out

00:59:44.999 --> 00:59:46.369
of four.

00:59:46.369 --> 00:59:51.089
Our recruiting pool is 25 percent of our total
population.

00:59:51.089 --> 00:59:57.259
They don't qualify because they didn't finish
high school.

00:59:57.259 --> 01:00:01.630
They don't qualify because of health issues,
mainly obesity.

01:00:01.630 --> 01:00:04.640
They don't qualify because they've got a criminal
record.

01:00:04.640 --> 01:00:09.210
We don't give any waivers to get into the
military.

01:00:09.210 --> 01:00:10.390
But that ought to frighten us.

01:00:10.390 --> 01:00:12.529
We ought to do a better job in terms of health
care.

01:00:12.529 --> 01:00:14.359
We ought to do a better job in terms of education.

01:00:14.359 --> 01:00:21.420
I mean, I spent a large part of my live working
on education in Mississippi.

01:00:21.420 --> 01:00:29.359
And I don't care if you don't have children,
I don't care if you don't have children in

01:00:29.359 --> 01:00:35.160
public schools, I don't care if your children
are out of school, you've got a vested interest

01:00:35.160 --> 01:00:37.609
in making sure that our public schools are
great.

01:00:37.609 --> 01:00:39.569
You just do.

01:00:39.569 --> 01:00:44.140
And I went and I made a speech to the Mississippi
legislature in 2011.

01:00:44.140 --> 01:00:52.260
It's the first time I talked to them since
I left office in 1992.

01:00:52.260 --> 01:00:56.779
I made the same speech in 2011 I did in 1992.

01:00:56.779 --> 01:01:02.369
I mean, I started out by saying, as I was
saying when I got interrupted.

01:01:02.369 --> 01:01:03.369
(Laughter.)

01:01:03.369 --> 01:01:09.319
But, you know, every one of those legislators
came up to me and said, what can we do to

01:01:09.319 --> 01:01:10.400
help the military?

01:01:10.400 --> 01:01:11.650
We want to help you.

01:01:11.650 --> 01:01:13.329
And I said, OK, you want to help us?

01:01:13.329 --> 01:01:14.820
Do better in education.

01:01:14.820 --> 01:01:16.119
You want to help us?

01:01:16.119 --> 01:01:17.869
Do better in health care.

01:01:17.869 --> 01:01:20.190
You want to help us?

01:01:20.190 --> 01:01:23.339
Keep people in school and out of jail.

01:01:23.339 --> 01:01:25.010
That's the way you can help the military.

01:01:25.010 --> 01:01:27.800
So I just think that's a false choice.

01:01:27.800 --> 01:01:30.190
I think we've got to-we've got to do both.

01:01:30.190 --> 01:01:34.130
JOSKOW: Well, on that note, the witching hour
is here.

01:01:34.130 --> 01:01:36.430
We end our Council meetings on time.

01:01:36.430 --> 01:01:46.339
I'd like-please give Secretary Mabus a round
of applause for a fascinating discussion.

01:01:46.339 --> 01:01:47.339
(Applause.)

01:01:47.339 --> 01:01:48.339
And thank you all for coming.

01:01:48.339 --> 01:01:51.700
I'm sorry we couldn't get to all the questions.

01:01:51.700 --> 01:01:51.790
(END)

